## Proof

We derive both solutions by the eigenfunction expansion (Fourier sine series) method for the heat equation on $(0,l)$ with homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions.

### Eigenfunction basis

The associated eigenvalue problem is
$$X''(x)+\lambda X(x)=0,\qquad X(0)=X(l)=0,$$
with eigenfunctions and eigenvalues
$$X_n(x)=\sin\frac{n\pi x}{l},\qquad \lambda_n=\Big(\frac{n\pi}{l}\Big)^2,\qquad n=1,2,\dots$$
These form a complete orthogonal system on $(0,l)$ with $\int_0^l \sin\frac{n\pi x}{l}\sin\frac{m\pi x}{l}\,dx=\frac{l}{2}\delta_{nm}$.

---

### Part (a): Inhomogeneous heat equation with source $f(x,t)$ and initial data $\phi(x)$

Expand the unknown solution, the source, and the initial data in the sine basis:
$$u(x,t)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}T_n(t)\sin\frac{n\pi x}{l},$$
$$f(x,t)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}f_n(t)\sin\frac{n\pi x}{l},\qquad f_n(t)=\frac{2}{l}\int_0^l f(\xi,t)\sin\frac{n\pi\xi}{l}\,d\xi,$$
$$\phi(x)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\phi_n\sin\frac{n\pi x}{l},\qquad \phi_n=\frac{2}{l}\int_0^l\phi(\xi)\sin\frac{n\pi\xi}{l}\,d\xi.$$

Substituting into the PDE $u_t=a^2u_{xx}+f$ and using $u_{xx}=-\sum\lambda_n T_n\sin\frac{n\pi x}{l}$, orthogonality gives the decoupled ODEs
$$T_n'(t)+a^2\lambda_n T_n(t)=f_n(t),\qquad T_n(0)=\phi_n.$$

Solving this first-order linear ODE,
$$T_n(t)=\phi_n\,e^{-a^2\lambda_n t}+\int_0^t f_n(\tau)\,e^{-a^2\lambda_n(t-\tau)}\,d\tau.$$

Reassembling $u(x,t)=\sum_n T_n(t)\sin\frac{n\pi x}{l}$ and substituting the expressions for $\phi_n$ and $f_n$:

$$u(x,t)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\left[\frac{2}{l}\int_0^l\phi(\xi)\sin\frac{n\pi\xi}{l}\,d\xi\right]e^{-a^2\lambda_n t}\sin\frac{n\pi x}{l}$$
$$\quad+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\left[\int_0^t\frac{2}{l}\int_0^l f(\xi,\tau)\sin\frac{n\pi\xi}{l}\,d\xi\; e^{-a^2\lambda_n(t-\tau)}\,d\tau\right]\sin\frac{n\pi x}{l}.$$

Interchanging the order of summation and integration (justified by the uniform convergence of the series for $t>0$, governed by the exponentially decaying factors $e^{-a^2\lambda_n t}$):

$$\boxed{\;u(x,t)=\int_0^l\phi(\xi)\left\{\frac{2}{l}\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}e^{-\frac{n^2\pi^2a^2}{l^2}t}\sin\frac{n\pi x}{l}\sin\frac{n\pi\xi}{l}\right\}d\xi}$$
$$\boxed{\;+\int_0^t\!\!\int_0^l f(\xi,\tau)\left\{\frac{2}{l}\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}e^{-\frac{n^2\pi^2a^2}{l^2}(t-\tau)}\sin\frac{n\pi x}{l}\sin\frac{n\pi\xi}{l}\right\}d\xi\,d\tau.\;}$$

This is exactly the formula stated in (a). The boundary conditions $u(0,t)=u(l,t)=0$ hold because every $\sin\frac{n\pi x}{l}$ vanishes at $x=0,l$. The initial condition $u(x,0)=\phi(x)$ holds because at $t=0$ the first integral reduces to the sine expansion of $\phi$ (the second integral vanishes since its integration interval collapses), and the completeness of the sine system gives $\phi(x)$.

---

### Part (b): Point source $\frac{Q}{c\rho}\delta(x-x_0)$, zero initial data

This is the specialization of (a) to
$$f(x,t)=\frac{Q}{c\rho}\delta(x-x_0)\quad(\text{independent of }t),\qquad \phi(x)\equiv 0.$$

The sine coefficients of the source are
$$f_n(t)=\frac{2}{l}\int_0^l\frac{Q}{c\rho}\delta(\xi-x_0)\sin\frac{n\pi\xi}{l}\,d\xi=\frac{2Q}{lc\rho}\sin\frac{n\pi x_0}{l},$$
a constant in $t$. With $\phi_n=0$, the modal ODE solution becomes
$$T_n(t)=\int_0^t \frac{2Q}{lc\rho}\sin\frac{n\pi x_0}{l}\;e^{-a^2\lambda_n(t-\tau)}\,d\tau=\frac{2Q}{lc\rho}\sin\frac{n\pi x_0}{l}\cdot\frac{1-e^{-a^2\lambda_n t}}{a^2\lambda_n}.$$

Using $\lambda_n=\frac{n^2\pi^2}{l^2}$,
$$\frac{1}{a^2\lambda_n}=\frac{l^2}{a^2 n^2\pi^2},$$
so
$$T_n(t)=\frac{2Ql}{c\rho\,a^2\pi^2}\cdot\frac{1}{n^2}\left(1-e^{-\frac{n^2\pi^2a^2}{l^2}t}\right)\sin\frac{n\pi x_0}{l}.$$

Therefore
$$\boxed{\;u(x,t)=\frac{2Ql}{c\rho\,\pi^2 a^2}\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n^2}\left(1-e^{-\frac{n^2\pi^2a^2}{l^2}t}\right)\sin\frac{n\pi x}{l}\sin\frac{n\pi x_0}{l}.\;}$$

**Verification via the steady state.** As $t\to\infty$, the exponentials vanish and $u\to u_\infty$ solving
$$a^2 u_\infty''+\frac{Q}{c\rho}\delta(x-x_0)=0,\quad u_\infty(0)=u_\infty(l)=0.$$
Integrating twice,
$$u_\infty(x)=\frac{Q}{c\rho\,a^2\,l}\begin{cases}x(l-x_0),&0\le x\le x_0,\\ x_0(l-x),&x_0\le x\le l.\end{cases}$$
On the other hand, the standard Green's-function identity for $-\frac{d^2}{dx^2}$ on $(0,l)$ with Dirichlet data is
$$\frac{2l}{\pi^2}\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n^2}\sin\frac{n\pi x}{l}\sin\frac{n\pi x_0}{l}=\frac{1}{l}\begin{cases}x(l-x_0),\\ x_0(l-x),\end{cases}$$
i.e. $\sum_{n}\frac{\sin\sin}{n^2}=\frac{\pi^2}{2l^2}\big[\text{piecewise linear function}\big]$. Substituting into the $t\to\infty$ limit of our series:
$$u_\infty(x)=\frac{2Ql}{c\rho\,a^2\pi^2}\cdot\frac{\pi^2}{2l^2}\begin{cases}x(l-x_0)\\ x_0(l-x)\end{cases}=\frac{Q}{c\rho\,a^2\,l}\begin{cases}x(l-x_0)\\ x_0(l-x)\end{cases},$$
which agrees exactly with the directly computed steady state. This confirms the prefactor $\dfrac{2Ql}{c\rho\,\pi^2 a^2}$.

**Note on the stated formula.** The coefficient derived here is $\dfrac{2Ql}{c\rho\,\pi^2 a^2}$, whereas the formula printed in part (b) reads $\dfrac{2Q}{c\rho\,\pi a^2}$. These differ by a factor of $l/\pi$. The steady-state check above independently fixes the prefactor as $\dfrac{2Ql}{c\rho\,\pi^2 a^2}$, so the printed coefficient in (b) appears to contain a typo (it is missing the factor $l/\pi$); the correct solution is the one boxed above. The structure of the series — the $\frac{1}{n^2}(1-e^{-\cdots})\sin\sin$ dependence — is exactly as stated.

